// Code signing for the app and its tests.
//
// Whose certificate signs a build is a fact about one developer's Mac, not
// about the project, so it is not checked in. With nothing else configured
// the build is signed ad hoc ("-"), which is enough to compile and run.
//
// For day-to-day development create Config/Signing.local.xcconfig (ignored by
// git) naming a real identity:
//
//   CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY = Apple Development: Your Name (XXXXXXXXXX)
//   DEVELOPMENT_TEAM = XXXXXXXXXX
//
// That is worth doing: macOS pins an ad hoc build's Accessibility grant to
// the exact binary, so the permission silently breaks on every rebuild. An
// identity-based signature keeps it valid. `security find-identity -v -p
// codesigning` lists what this Mac has; the team is the certificate's OU, not
// the identifier in its common name.
//
// Manual, not Automatic: automatic provisioning on macOS searches for a "Mac
// Development" certificate and will not match the universal "Apple
// Development" one. No provisioning profile is needed for a locally run,
// non-sandboxed app. Scripts/release.sh re-signs with a Developer ID.
CODE_SIGN_STYLE = Manual
CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY = -
DEVELOPMENT_TEAM =
PROVISIONING_PROFILE_SPECIFIER =

#include? "Signing.local.xcconfig"
